[agent] ## What I did (all via the `databricks` CLI)
[agent] 1. **Registered model** — created `workspace.mlpab8de01b.churnmodel7948d0` in the per-run UC schema (`databricks registered-models create`).
[agent] 2. **Artifact as content** — created a managed UC volume `workspace.mlpab8de01b.churnartifacts`, uploaded `data/model.json` to it, then created **version 1** of the model from that volume source via the MLflow-UC REST endpoint (`model-versions/create`) and finalized it → status **READY** (the artifact file is the version's content).
[agent] 3. **Metrics attached two ways** for robustness:
[agent]    - Logged `auc=0.8524` / `accuracy=0.7254` to an MLflow **run** (`experiments log-metric`) under `/Users/benedict@logicalclocks.com/mlpab8de01b/`, and linked that run to the model version via `run_id`.
[agent]    - Set them as **tags** directly on model version 1 (`model-versions/set-tag`).
[agent] 4. **Deliverable** — wrote `submission/answers.json`:
[agent]    ```json
[agent]    {"model_name": "churnmodel7948d0", "version": 1, "metrics": {"auc": 0.8524, "accuracy": 0.7254}}
[agent]    ```
[agent] Verified by reading back through the platform: registered model exists, version 1 is `READY`, and the metrics are retrievable from both the linked run and the version tags.
[agent] One note on the interface: the CLI's `model-versions` surface has no `create` command (it documents that "creating model versions requires the MLflow Python client"), so version creation had to go through `databricks api post` to the MLflow-UC REST endpoints. A managed-volume `source` let the server-side `finalize` copy the artifact without any local cloud-storage upload, which kept everything on the CLI.
